---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Furnace Blowing Cold Air? What to Check First"
  description="Furnace running but blowing cold air? The five most common causes, what you can safely check yourself, and when to stop and call for repair."
>

<section class="blog-post-hero">

  <div class="container">

    <div class="post-meta">
      <span class="category">Troubleshooting</span>
      <span class="dot">•</span>
      <span>January 14, 2026</span>
    </div>

    <h1>
      Why Your Furnace Is Blowing Cold Air,
      <span>and What to Check First</span>
    </h1>

    <p class="lead">
      Furnace running but blowing cold air? Learn the five most common causes,
      what you can safely check yourself, and when it's time to call a heating
      repair professional.
    </p>

    <img
      src="https://assets.cdn.filesafe.space/yYhT9V8bWmH856u4w7q3/media/6a733fccee50b30ddcdd755e.png"
      alt="Hand held in front of a floor heat register checking airflow temperature"
    />

  </div>

</section>

<section class="blog-content">

  <div class="container article">

    <p>
      You set the thermostat, the furnace comes on, and what comes out of the vents is cool. Not freezing, usually. Just distinctly not heat. It is one of the most common calls we take in Whatcom County between October and March, and it is worth knowing that it is very often one of the cheapest problems on the list.
    </p>

    <p>
      Here is what is actually happening, in the order we check it.
    </p>

    <h2>Start with the thermostat fan setting</h2>

    <p>
      Look at your thermostat and find the fan setting. If it is set to <strong>On</strong> rather than <strong>Auto</strong>, that is very likely your answer, and it costs nothing to fix.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Quick Tip</strong>
      <p>
        Set the fan to <strong>Auto</strong> so the blower only runs while the furnace is producing heat.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p>
      On means the blower runs continuously whether or not the burner is lit. For part of the hour you get warm air, and the rest of the time you get room-temperature air, which feels cold against your skin.
    </p>

    <h2>Then check whether it is short cycling</h2>

    <p>
      Stand near the furnace and watch a full run. If it lights, runs for a few minutes, shuts down, waits, and tries again, your furnace is short cycling.
    </p>

    <p>
      The most common cause is a dirty flame sensor. After a few seasons it builds up a film and can no longer detect the burner flame correctly, causing the furnace to shut itself down as a safety precaution.
    </p>

    <div class="callout warning">
      <strong>Good News</strong>
      <p>
        Cleaning or replacing a flame sensor is one of the least expensive furnace repairs.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Check the filter</h2>

    <p>
      A clogged filter restricts airflow. The furnace overheats, the high-limit switch turns the burner off, and the blower continues running, sending cool air through the vents.
    </p>

    <p>
      Hold the filter up to a light. If light cannot pass through it, replace it with a new one of the same size.
    </p>

  </div>

</section>

</BaseLayout>

